This is a unit test as a contract test for ArrayList.IndexOf, it assumes the functionality of ArrayList and tries to test the contract for this method. It also observes several properties of the ArrayList for visualization in the Pex session. And finally, there are a bunch of assertions to test the invariants of the method.

All these concepts are covered in the Microsoft Pex and Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA) framework for unit testing. Make sure you understand the AAA framework before you get started with Microsoft's Pex tools.

Also remember that you generally need to generate multiple permutations to fully test your contract, using PexInvariant attributes to describe your contract, and PexObserve attributes to store the results of your tests for viewing in a Pex session.

Here are the basics: 

- PexArrange attribute: it is for the Arrange stage of testing. Here you set-up the state for some method under test (method being tested) before it runs. Basically, we initialize an ArrayList object and insert some integer values into it. 

- PexAct attribute: it is for the Act stage of testing. It is run after Arrange where you call the method you're testing. Here we use IndexOf method for our ArrayList.

- PexAssert attribute: to verify the expected outcome of the test. In this case, we check that the IndexOf method returns a  valid index of our inserted integer value.

- PexInvariant and PexObserve: are for storing the results of the tests for viewing in a Pex session. PexInvariant is used to declare a custom invariant to be checked by Pex, and PexObserve is used to store the value of a state variable you’d like observed in the Pex session.

Hope this was helpful to understand Microsoft's Pex and Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA) framework for unit testing.

Note: The above is a general guidance, you might need to modify the test according to your specifications. 

If you've any questions please do not hesitate to ask.


