Questions and Answers

1. What is the purpose of the PexArrange, PexAct, PexAssert, PexInvariant, and PexObserve attributes? What do they allow you to test for?

PexArrange, PexAct, and PexAssert are commonly used in the Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA) testing pattern. The Arrange step is to setup the initial state for the--------------------	    bool isNegative = New_Ret < 0; 
	    bool oldLastIndexOfXIsNegative = Old_arrListLastIndexOfX < 0;
	    Assert.IsTrue(isNegative == oldLastIndexOfXIsNegative);

            AssumePostcondition.IsTrue(true);
        }
    }
}

//ArrayList.Test/ArrayListContractTest/PUT_RemoveContract.cs
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework.Stubs;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework.Validation;
using System;
using System.Collections;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Text;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_RemoveContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int index)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index >= 0 && index < arrList.Count, arrList);

            object Old_Value = arrList[index];
            bool Old_arrListContainsValue = arrList.Contains(Old_Value);
            int Old_x = (int)Old_Value;
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(Old_x);
            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;

            object New_Value = arrList.Remove(index);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            bool New_arrListContainsValue = arrList.Contains(Old_Value);
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(Old_x);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_value", Old_Value);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_value", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Contains_value", New_arrListContainsValue);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_value", New_Value);

            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListContainsValue == false);
	    Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX == -1 || (Old_arrListLastIndexOfX >= 0 && Old_arrListLastIndexOfX == arrList.LastIndexOf(Old_x)));
	    Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListLastIndexOfX == -1 || (New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= 0 && New_arrListLastIndexOfX <= index));
	    Assert.AreEqual(New_Value, Old_Value);

            AssumePostcondition.IsTrue(arrList.Count == Old_arrListCount - 1);
        }
    }
}

//ArrayList.Test/ArrayListContractTest/PUT_SetItemContract.cs
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework.Stubs;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework.Validation;
using System;
using System.Collections;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Text;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_SetItemContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int index, object oldValue)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index >= 0 && index < arrList.Count, arrList);

            object New_Value = arrList[index];

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_value", oldValue);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_value", New_Value);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$index", index);

            Assert.IsTrue(New_Value == oldValue);

            AssumePostcondition.IsTrue(true);
        }
    }
}

//ArrayList.Test/ArrayListTest/ArrayListTest.cs
using ArrayList.Library;
using NUnit.Framework;
using System;
using System.Collections;

namespace ArrayList.--------------------	    Debug.Assert(!(New_Ret < 0 && Old_arrListLastIndexOfX == -1));

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ If the return value is non-negative, new index of x is equal to old index of x 
	    Debug.Assert(!(New_Ret >= 0 && Old_arrListContainsX ? New_Ret == Old_arrListLastIndexOfX : true));
        }
    }
}

```

The above test checks if `IndexOf` returns the correct index if the element is present in the list. 

* The arraylist's Count should remain the same before and after calling IndexOf method, because `IndexOf` just works on searching for elements in the list and doesn't change its size.
* The index returned by `IndexOf` operation should be the same as the last index of the element in the arraylist `x` before the operation.
* If `x` is in the arraylist before the operation, the ArrayList contains x after the IndexOf operation should stay `true`.
* If the return index from IndexOf is not negative(-1), then the index of `x` should be same as the last index of `x` in the arraylist before IndexOf was called.
* If the `x` is there in the arraylist before the operation, the ArrayList contains x after IndexOf operation is `True`.

Please note, Pex has limitation in analysing dynamic data so if there is any dynamic operation then you need to create multiple tests.

Pex has two types of constraints:

* Postconditions which ensure the method behaviours as it should.
* Preconditions which ensure that the method will operate correctly when given certain input.

In this case, we wrote POSTCONDITION for the `IndexOf` method .

You can find all code fragments under Test -> Test Explorer -> Verified –> [Your Test Case Name]

This will show all steps that were executed while the test was running.

This includes things like Assert Results which could give some sort of output at the end saying what was right and what was not.

If you can see a message saying the tests have been run successfully then all your tests are working properly.

A few tips while testing:

* Ensure you create sufficient data for testing
* Try to cover all possible scenarios
* Validate data constraints such as not null or valid range
* Do not assume any behaviour to be 'correct' under normal day-to-day conditions

In most cases, testing is done manually, automated, and model-based. Testing the contract guarantees that the method does what it states it does. The contract also ensures that the code complies with design requirements.

In conclusion, Automated tests help in quickly identifying any issues while manual testing would have taken time to go through all possibilities.

NOTE: The actual test cases and their results depend upon data and scenario specifics of the program.

This sample code tested a few simple preconditions and postconditions of ArrayList’s IndexOf method. In reality, you would want to include more robust and specific testing. 

For instance, you may want to test what happens if you use IndexOf on a non-existing element, test if ArrayList is empty etc. Please remember that testing is about understanding and validating code behaves as expected, so you want comprehensive tests. It's often wise to use both automated and manual testing to ensure the robustness of your code.

It's important to comment all your logic, not just your code where there are possible failures. This makes your code easier to maintain and review.

Remember, unit testing is not just black-box testing. We also test ArrayList's internal working through its fields. We test each method's return values by validating them by comparing with expected result obtained from debug process. We write assert statements, which compare the expected output with the actual output. If the expected output does not match the actual, our test has failed. This is how manual testing works.

Also there are other testing methodologies like White box testing, grey box testing, and black box testing, which have more nuanced aspects. But now let's shift to white box testing. It is also known as glass box testing where we know about the internal structure of system and testing the functionality of system manually with the help of testing methods.

Hope this sample makes you understand about unit testing and Pex tool a bit more. Let me know if you have more doubts.

Happy coding!!


